KNOWNBUG
main.cpp
--cpp11
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ class with private DMI-bearing member default-constructs: SUCCESS$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
--
Currently: "member 'goto_statet::propagation' is not accessible
(private)" followed by "default constructor of 'struct goto_statet' is
not accessible".  Removing the member's default member initializer, or
making the member public, avoids it.
ROUND-97 FIX ATTEMPT AND ITS CONSTRAINT (recorded so it is not
repeated naively): disabling access control while converting a body
marked #is_implicit_ctor / #is_implicit_dtor (a guard in
convert_function) DOES fix this kernel and lets
src/goto-symex/symex_throw.cpp produce a goto binary -- but it is TOO
COARSE and broke regression/cpp/Protection1, a rejects-invalid test:
    class A { int i; A(int); private: A(); };
    class B : A {};   B b;   // must be REJECTED
Within B's implicit default constructor, B's OWN private members are
accessible, but A's PRIVATE default constructor is not
([class.access.general]/4 grants the class's own context, not its
bases').  A correct fix must therefore judge access in the context of
the class that DECLARES the entity -- e.g. by setting
access_judgment_scope to the constructed class's scope (the mechanism
round 74 used for friend declarations) rather than switching access
checking off.  Verified: with the coarse guard reverted, Protection1
passes again.
